WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.850
Take two metal plates and empty out everything between them: no air, no charge, no magnetic field.

2
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:12.580
Bring them within a micron of each other and the plates begin to be pushed toward one another.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.590
In the middle of nothingness, what is pushing them?

4
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.000
Picture two metal plates.

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.490
Flat as mirrors, facing one another.

6
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:33.300
Both are electrically neutral, neither one is a magnet.

7
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.990
Now pump out all the air between them; leave behind not a speck of dust, not a single molecule of gas.

8
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:45.080
By textbook logic, these two plates have nothing whatsoever to say to each other.

9
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.690
No charge, no current, no matter, and therefore no force.

10
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:55.420
And yet, when you shrink the gap between them far enough, the plates begin to be drawn together.

11
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.590
Something is pressing in on them.

12
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:04.640
And the name of that something, at least as the story was first told, is nothingness itself.

13
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:11.170
The place where this idea was born was not a theoretical institute but an industrial laboratory.

14
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:20.260
In nineteen forty-eight, Hendrik Casimir was working on an utterly ordinary problem at the research laboratory of the Dutch electronics company Philips: colloids.

15
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:28.390
That is, the question of why very small particles suspended in a liquid sometimes stay dispersed and sometimes clump together and sink to the bottom.

16
00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:36.360
The company's interest in the question was not philosophical but commercial; half of its products depended on suspensions of exactly this kind.

17
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:46.730
The force pulling the particles toward each other was already known: even in uncharged molecules, the distribution of charge inside shifts one way for an instant, then the other,

18
00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:51.820
and this momentary imbalance provokes a response in a neighbour, so the two attract.

19
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:59.790
When Casimir, together with his young colleague Dirk Polder, carried the calculation a little further, he found something strange.

20
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.720
As the distance grew, the attraction died away faster than expected.

21
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.970
The reason was simple but subtle: electromagnetic influence travels at the speed of light.

22
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:16.980
By the time one molecule's message reaches its neighbour, the sending molecule has already moved on to some other state.

23
00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.590
When the reply comes back, the coordination has broken down.

24
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:28.920
Because of this delay, the interaction between two atoms weakened not with the sixth power of the distance, but with the seventh.

25
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.890
What was truly unsettling, though, was the shape of the result.

26
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:41.500
The expression that emerged was astonishingly clean; none of the messy details of the atom survived in it, only Planck's constant and the speed of light.

27
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:46.910
Recounting it years later, Casimir said that this very cleanness had left him uneasy.

28
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.040
During a walk with Niels Bohr, he described the matter.

29
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.930
Bohr, as if muttering to himself, said that it must have something to do with zero point energy.

30
00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.900
That single sentence rebuilt the problem from the ground up.

31
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.670
Because according to quantum field theory, empty space is not empty.

32
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:18.760
The electromagnetic field exists at every point in space, and every possible mode of vibration of that field, even at absolute zero, never comes fully to rest; an energy remains that cannot be reduced to zero.

33
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:24.970
The vacuum is not the name of nothingness; it is the name of a field's lowest energy state.

34
00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.340
And that state trembles.

35
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.950
Casimir took the problem away from atoms and handed it directly to the field.

36
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:42.000
Only certain wavelengths fit between two conducting plates; just like a guitar string fixed at both ends, only the vibrations that fit exactly can survive.

37
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.770
Outside the plates there is no such restriction; every wavelength is free.

38
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.220
So the variety of vibration inside is sparser than it is outside.

39
00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:58.390
The surplus pressing in from outside is greater than what pushes back from within, and the plates are dragged toward one another.

40
00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:09.080
The numerical form of the result is just as spare: the force per unit area depends only on Planck's constant, on the speed of light, and on the fourth power of the distance between the plates.

41
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.250
No material, no charge.

42
00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:17.340
That fourth power is what creates the real drama in this story.

43
00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.750
At a gap of one micrometre the force is so small that measuring it is next to impossible.

44
00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:32.960
Drop the gap to ten nanometres, that is, shorten the distance by a factor of one hundred, and the pressure rises by a factor of one hundred million, reaching the order of one atmosphere:

45
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.930
the very air pressure bearing down on your shoulders at sea level.

46
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.740
Nothingness, at the right scale, behaves like tonnes of weight.

47
00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:49.990
Sailors had known of an observation resembling this effect for centuries: in a heavy swell, two ships lying side by side are drawn toward each other,

48
00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:57.320
because in the narrow stretch of water between the hulls the play of the waves is restricted, while the open sea outside is unbounded.

49
00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:01.930
The analogy is not perfect, but it puts the intuition in the right place.

50
00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:05.740
Measurement, however, was a craft all its own.

51
00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:12.910
The first attempt came in nineteen fifty-eight, from Marcus Sparnaay, who worked in that same Philips laboratory.

52
00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:23.120
Using a spring balance he caught a force, and reported that what he had found did not contradict Casimir's prediction; but in practice the uncertainty of his measurement was one hundred percent.

53
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:32.370
Holding two plates perfectly parallel, and cleaning off the stray charges that lingered on them, produced errors so enormous that they swamped the very force he was hunting.

54
00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.380
Real proof came forty years later.

55
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:46.710
In nineteen ninety-seven, at the University of Washington, Steve Lamoreaux resorted to a trick that cut the parallelism problem out at the root: instead of a flat plate, he used a sphere,

56
00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:51.480
because the closest point between a sphere and a plane is single and well defined.

57
00:05:51.480 --> 00:06:00.410
With a torsion pendulum he measured across separations from zero point six to six micrometres, and found agreement with theory at the level of five percent.

58
00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:13.500
A year later, at the University of California's Riverside campus, Umar Mohideen and Anushree Roy used an atomic force microscope to push the measurement, over the range from zero point one to zero point nine micrometres,

59
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.870
down to the one percent level.

60
00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.680
The debate was no longer about whether the force existed.

61
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.010
The debate was about whose problem it was.

62
00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:30.020
Because by the nineteen nineties the Casimir force had stopped being a laboratory curiosity and had become an engineering obstacle.

63
00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:36.550
Micromachines, carved into silicon wafers by chemical etching, had entered everyday life in those years.

64
00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:48.200
The accelerometers that trigger a car's airbag, the gyroscopes that know which way a phone has been turned, the arrays inside projectors made of millions of tiny mirrors each only a few micrometres across.

65
00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:54.090
They all shared one chronic ailment: once a moving part touched its neighbour, it never came back.

66
00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.780
It stuck.

67
00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:06.670
Some of the culprits were familiar: the capillary pull of the thin film of water left on the surface, stray electrostatic charges, short-range molecular attraction.

68
00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:14.640
But when the gaps dropped below one hundred nanometres, one more item joined the list, and this item grew as the fourth power of the distance.

69
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.330
Sticking was not the only problem.

70
00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:24.500
When you bring a spring-held part close to its neighbour, if the attractive force grows faster than the spring's restoring force,

71
00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:30.390
a point of instability is crossed and the part collapses irreversibly onto the opposing surface.

72
00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:37.400
From then on, anyone designing a micromachine had to enter a term from quantum field theory into their force budget.

73
00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:46.490
In two thousand one, Ho Bun Chan and his colleagues, on a team that included Federico Capasso, turned this relationship around and put it on display.

74
00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:53.820
They built a tiny plate of polysilicon suspended from two thin torsion rods, and brought a metal-coated sphere close to it.

75
00:07:53.820 --> 00:08:01.630
As the sphere approached, the plate rotated, and the way the rotation angle depended on distance matched the Casimir calculation.

76
00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:07.040
The meaning was plain: the fluctuations of the vacuum were now moving a machine part.

77
00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.370
The effect could become part of the design.

78
00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:13.380
The next question was unavoidable.

79
00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:18.310
If attraction glues parts together, could repulsion hold them apart in mid-air?

80
00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.920
A frictionless nanoscale bearing; gears that never touch one another.

81
00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:27.850
Theory's first answers were tantalizing, but not something you could hold in your hand.

82
00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:39.500
In nineteen sixty-eight, Timothy Boyer calculated that a spherical shell of perfect conductor would be pushed outward rather than collapse inward; geometry, it turned out, could flip the sign.

83
00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:47.790
And in nineteen seventy-four he showed that placing a perfect magnetic mirror opposite a perfect electric mirror would give rise to repulsion.

84
00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:52.720
But no such material existed, and there still isn't one.

85
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:58.610
The realistic route ran through Evgeny Lifshitz's general theory of nineteen fifty-six.

86
00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:10.420
Lifshitz dropped Casimir's assumption of flawless mirrors and put in its place the real, frequency-dependent electrical response of the material; Casimir's clean formula was one idealized limit of this broader theory.

87
00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:18.230
A few years later, together with his colleagues, he derived the following condition: if you place a liquid between two solids,

88
00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:26.840
and the liquid's electromagnetic response falls right between those of the two solids over the relevant range of frequencies, the sign of the force reverses.

89
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:33.690
In two thousand nine, Jeremy Munday, Federico Capasso and Adrian Parsegian put this to the test.

90
00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:41.340
A gold sphere, a plate of silica, that is, silicon dioxide, and between the two a liquid called bromobenzene.

91
00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:47.070
Gold's response lay above that of bromobenzene, and bromobenzene's above that of silica.

92
00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:57.920
What made the measurement difficult was the fluid drag the sphere felt as it moved through the liquid; the team repeated the experiment at different approach speeds to strip out the drag force, which depends on speed.

93
00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.530
What remained was a force on the order of piconewtons, and it was repulsive.

94
00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:06.340
The sphere was being pushed away from the plate.

95
00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:11.430
And this is exactly where the tension begins, because this victory has narrow limits.

96
00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:16.200
Repulsion requires a liquid; in dry vacuum the job is far harder.

97
00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:25.290
In two thousand six, Oded Kenneth and Israel Klich proved that in vacuum two bodies that are mirror images of each other will always attract, whatever they are made of.

98
00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:32.460
Which means the door was theoretically closed on the idea of pushing two identical parts apart in vacuum and holding them in mid-air.

99
00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.790
The remaining road ran through asymmetry.

100
00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:47.120
In twenty nineteen, Rongkuo Zhao and his colleagues, in a study that Capasso also took part in, coated one of two gold surfaces with a thin layer of Teflon and immersed both of them in ethanol.

101
00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:53.170
The result was a hybrid behaviour: attraction at long range, repulsion at short range.

102
00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:58.420
Where the two curves crossed, a stable equilibrium point was born.

103
00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:05.910
The object hung suspended, at a gap on the order of a hundred nanometres, with no energy supplied from outside at all.

104
00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:11.960
A platform was proposed for contactless nanomachines and highly sensitive force detectors.

105
00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:18.490
To be honest, this is still a laboratory demonstration; not in a product, not on a production line.

106
00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:23.260
Now let us come to the uncomfortable question that lies beneath this whole story.

107
00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:28.350
Up to this point I have described everything as the pressure of the fluctuations of the vacuum.

108
00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:32.800
But do these experiments really prove that empty space carries energy?

109
00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:39.970
In two thousand five, Robert Jaffe of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology took direct aim at this assumption.

110
00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:47.780
What he showed was this: the Casimir force can be derived from beginning to end without any mention whatsoever of zero point energy.

111
00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:59.590
In Lifshitz's framework the force arises from the fluctuation of the charges and the currents inside the plates; the quantum jitter of the electrons in the metal speaks, by way of retarded electromagnetic fields,

112
00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:02.120
with the jitter in the opposing plate.

113
00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:06.890
You are not obliged to attribute any energy to the empty space in between.

114
00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:11.180
The criterion Jaffe offers is persuasive.

115
00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:17.390
The force depends on the fine structure constant, the number that tells you how strongly matter couples to light.

116
00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:26.000
When you turn that coupling down in thought, the force disappears; whereas the zero point energy of the vacuum would, by definition, go on sitting right there.

117
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:34.130
What is more, the measurements too are sensitive to the material in a way that supports this: real metals become transparent at high frequencies,

118
00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:40.340
and in order to seat the experiment on the theory it is essential to account for the optical data of the material.

119
00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:49.110
Casimir's elegant formula, made up of only two fundamental constants, is an idealization that has never been observed directly in any laboratory.

120
00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:59.960
Today the great majority of physicists see these two accounts not as contradictory, but as two languages writing the same physics into different ledgers; the calculations give the same number.

121
00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:05.530
But the sentence that this experiment proved the energy of the vacuum says more than the data permit.

122
00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:12.380
What the Casimir experiment measures is a difference: how the energy changes when you bring the plates closer together.

123
00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:16.190
About the absolute level it says not a single word.

124
00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.200
The real open wound stands exactly there.

125
00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:29.090
If you set out to sum the zero point energies of the quantum fields up to any reasonable upper limit, what comes out is an energy density of unimaginable size for empty space.

126
00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:37.060
Since gravity responds to energy, at that density the universe ought long ago to have collapsed in upon itself or been torn to pieces.

127
00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:46.790
And yet the density of the dark energy that is accelerating the expansion of the universe is ridiculously small; on the order of one billionth of a joule per cubic metre.

128
00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:55.240
The chasm between the two numbers is quoted as lying somewhere between sixty and a hundred and twenty orders of magnitude, depending on which cutoff you choose,

129
00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:59.370
and it is spoken of as the worst theoretical prediction in physics.

130
00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:05.260
The Casimir effect does not heal this wound, because it measures differences, not the level.

131
00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:10.670
Even so, the reality of quantum fluctuations is firmly supported from elsewhere.

132
00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:19.760
In nineteen forty-seven, Willis Lamb measured a minuscule splitting between two levels of the hydrogen atom that were expected to be equal in energy;

133
00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:26.130
that splitting was the direct signature of the electron interacting with the fluctuations of the field.

134
00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:35.060
And the tiny departure of the electron's magnetic moment from the value expected of it went on to become one of the most delicately tested predictions in all of physics.

135
00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:39.030
The most striking result came in twenty eleven.

136
00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:48.600
Back in nineteen seventy, Gerald Moore had predicted that if you could shake a mirror with an acceleration approaching the speed of light, you could tear real photons out of the vacuum;

137
00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:52.730
the trouble was that no mirror can be rattled anywhere near that fast.

138
00:14:52.730 --> 00:15:01.660
A team in Sweden, instead of moving a mirror physically, changed the electrical boundary condition of a superconducting circuit billions of times a second.

139
00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:07.070
The effective mirror was oscillating at a speed on the order of a few percent of the speed of light.

140
00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:12.160
Out of the circuit, with no source placed inside it, came microwave photons.

141
00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.170
Light torn out of the vacuum.

142
00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:22.020
The wrong conclusion to draw from this is already waiting, so it is worth saying plainly: this is not free energy.

143
00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:30.630
The Casimir force does work once, as the plates draw together, but in order to pull them apart again you have to give back exactly the same amount of work.

144
00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:32.680
The ledger closes.

145
00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:38.890
None of the contraptions that promise to draw limitless energy from the vacuum has any counterpart in physics.

146
00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:43.020
The lesson that remains is quieter, but deeper.

147
00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:50.350
What the Casimir effect shows is that the vacuum is not a neutral stage upon which events take place; it is among the actors.

148
00:15:50.350 --> 00:16:01.200
Nothingness is not an absolute thing, but a state defined by its boundaries; when you change the boundary by bringing two plates closer together, you have also changed what the nothingness between them is.

149
00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:05.170
And this is no longer merely a debate about concepts.

150
00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:16.020
An engineer designing a device that works at gaps of tens of nanometres, having written gravity, friction and electrostatics into the force budget, is obliged to open one more line.

151
00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:19.030
On that line there is nothing.

152
00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:22.360
And nothing, at that scale, pushes back.

153
00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:26.010
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

154
00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:30.300
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
